% ============================================================================
% celestial-object-alt-name — an alternate name NASA gives a basic celestial
% object, where the source names one (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `celestial-objects.adj`
% (`celestial_property(object, property)`, ONE defining property per object):
% a new `celestial_object_alt_name(object, alt_name)` table names an
% ALTERNATE NAME the source uses for an object, decoded from spans already
% sitting unused inside `celestial-objects.adj`'s own provenance block —
% no new WebFetch. That table's `property` column has room for only ONE
% defining fact per object; it already spent moon's slot on `orbits_planet`
% and asteroid's on `rocky`, so the synonyms the SAME already-quoted NASA
% sentences also state were left un-tabled:
%
%   moon → planetary_satellites
%     "Naturally-formed bodies that orbit planets are called moons, or
%      planetary satellites."
%   asteroid → minor_planets
%     "Asteroids, sometimes called minor planets, are rocky, airless
%      remnants left over from the early formation of our solar system
%      about 4.6 billion years ago."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? celestial_object_alt_name(moon, $AltName)      % planetary_satellites
%     ? celestial_object_alt_name(asteroid, $AltName)  % minor_planets
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `celestial-objects.adj`'s own `celestial_property`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `celestial_object_alt_name(object, alt_name)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the alternate name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any object the source does not give a second
% name for (`star`, `planet`, and `comet`'s cited NASA spans name only one
% term each — no alternate name to decode).
%
% Only these TWO of `celestial-objects.adj`'s five basic types have an
% alternate name in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is deliberately
% narrow rather than inventing synonyms the source does not state.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two spans already quoted
% inside `celestial-objects.adj`'s own provenance block, from the SAME NASA
% Science pages that table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a part of each already-verified quote. An ADJ
% `table` carries ONE provenance envelope (per `celestial-objects.adj`'s own
% convention), so `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the moon span; the
% asteroid row's own span and locator are listed above for independent
% auditability. `trust authoritative` — the same tier `celestial-objects.adj`
% already carries (NASA Science, a primary U.S. government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table celestial_object_alt_name {
    columns object, alt_name

    row (moon, planetary_satellites)
    row (asteroid, minor_planets)

    source "Naturally-formed bodies that orbit planets are called moons, or planetary satellites."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/"
    trust authoritative
}
